# The second brief, answered the obvious way: a line, and a drive train for it.
#
# The whole lesson of this design is in two words on one component. A crusher
# will not take a drive turning at speed 6, and a mill will not take one turning
# at speed 1, so a motor cannot drive both. Two trains, one geared down and one
# not, and the gearbox is the only part of the machine that does nothing to the
# ore.
#
# The mill is the bottleneck and says so: two motors on a shared shaft give it
# 67 rotary of the 80 it wants, so the line runs at 80/tick rather than 100 and
# everything upstream backs up behind it.
#
# Experiment 10 found something else wrong with it, which is the first time a
# design in this repository has been contradicted by its own geometry.
#
# `MO2.rotary -> SH1.in` has no valid route. Both motors are bolted to the same
# end of the same shaft, and only one of them can be: a coupling is a straight
# line between two flanges, MO1 is already on that line, and MO2 is three and a
# half metres to the south of it. Experiment 08 drew it anyway -- a shaft with
# two right angles in it, arriving at a flange sideways -- and nobody noticed
# for two experiments, which is exactly the class of defect the note said would
# start undermining the mechanic.
#
# It is left here rather than fixed. `machine space designs/07-crushline.machine`
# says so in one line, the plant has a visible hole where the drive should be,
# and the fix is a thing the player does: give the second motor its own shaft,
# or move it onto the line of the first. That is the whole argument for letting
# the router refuse.

machine "Crush Line"
brief crush

inlet     I1  at 0,4   draws ore

crusher   C1  at 0,0
crusher   C2  at 4,0
mill      MI1 at 9,0
separator S1  at 14,0
outlet    O1  at 19,0
skip      SK1 at 19,3

gearbox   GB1 at 0,7   ratio 4
shaft     SH1 at 2,10
shaft     SH2 at 12,6

mains     M1  at 10,9
motor     MO1 at 6,9
motor     MO2 at 6,12
motor     MO3 at 14,9
motor     MO4 at 14,12

wire M1.power -> MO1.power
wire M1.power -> MO2.power
wire M1.power -> MO3.power
wire M1.power -> MO4.power

wire MO1.rotary -> SH1.in
wire MO2.rotary -> SH1.in
wire SH1.out -> GB1.in
wire GB1.out -> C1.drive
wire GB1.out -> C2.drive

wire MO3.rotary -> SH2.in
wire MO4.rotary -> SH2.in
wire SH2.out -> MI1.drive
wire SH2.out -> S1.drive

wire I1.out -> C1.in
wire C1.out -> C2.in
wire C2.out -> MI1.in
wire MI1.out -> S1.in
wire S1.rich -> O1.solid
wire S1.tails -> SK1.solid
